# ############################################################################
# NOT ACTIVE ON THE CURRENT HOST.
#
# The site is served by Vercel, which reads redirects from vercel.json ONLY.
# It ignores this file entirely (this is Netlify/Cloudflare Pages format), and
# ignores .htaccess (Apache format). Every rule below was silently dead until
# it was ported into vercel.json.
#
# ==> Edit aog-static/vercel.json. Mirror the change here only to keep this
#     file useful if the site ever moves to Netlify or Cloudflare Pages.
#
# One rule could NOT be ported: /products/* returned 410 Gone here. Vercel's
# redirects only emit 3xx, so those spam URLs now return a plain 404. Google
# still drops them, just more slowly than with a 410.
# ############################################################################

# Host-level redirects (Netlify / Cloudflare Pages format).
# The static site keeps the same URLs as the old WordPress site, so most
# links carry over 1:1 with no redirect needed. Add old->new mappings here
# only when a URL actually changes during the content overhaul.
#
# Format:  /old-path   /new-path   301
#
# Example:
# /christmas-offer-2019/   /   301

# --- WordPress media ---------------------------------------------------------
# Images moved out of the WordPress library into /assets/media/. Page URLs are
# unchanged, but years of inbound links, Google Image results and old social
# cards still point at /wp-content/uploads/..., so keep those alive.
/wp-content/uploads/*   /assets/media/:splat            301
/wp-content/plugins/*   /assets/media/_plugins/:splat   301

# --- Retired WordPress endpoints ---------------------------------------------
# Nothing serves these any more; send them home rather than to a 404.
/wp-login.php           /   301
/wp-admin/*             /   301
/xmlrpc.php             /   301

# --- WordPress archive URLs --------------------------------------------------
# WordPress serves these even though they were never in the sitemap, and they
# currently return 200 — so Google has them indexed and they may carry links:
#   /category/<x>/   /tag/<x>/   /author/<x>/   /blog/page/2/   /2019/06/
# The static site generates no archives, so without these they would all 404 and
# any accumulated link equity would be thrown away. Sent to the blog index,
# which lists the posts those archives used to group.
/category/*             /blog/      301
/tag/*                  /blog/      301
/author/*               /blog/      301
/blog/page/*            /blog/      301
# Date archives (/2019/, /2019/06/). No real page starts with "20", so the
# wildcard is safe — verified against the built output.
/20*                    /blog/      301

# --- Injected spam URLs ------------------------------------------------------
# The compromised WordPress site served spam product pages on any unrecognised
# URL, and Google has INDEXED them (confirmed in Search Console, last crawled
# 25 Jul 2026): /products/<anything>?srsltid=...
#
# 410 Gone, not 404 and definitely not a redirect home. 410 states the page is
# permanently gone, which Google acts on faster than a 404; redirecting them to
# the homepage would instead read as a soft-404 and associate the spam with it.
#
# Do NOT add these to robots.txt — blocking the crawl would stop Google ever
# seeing the 410, and the URLs would linger in the index indefinitely.
/products/*             /404.html   410

